    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d345">

                <pb facs="0405" n="391" sameAs="frus:frus1944v07/pg391"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.35112A/559: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Argentina</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Armour</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Buenos Aires</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-01-04T18:00:00-03:00">January 4, 1944—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 6:40 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>17. Reference Embassy telegram No. 2850 of December 8, 5 p.m.<anchor n="49"
                        xml:id="d345-f1"/>
                </p>
                <pb facs="0406" n="392" xml:id="pg392"/>
                <p>Following fruitless efforts to secure funds for future operations from Banco di
                    Napoli, Italcable<anchor n="51" xml:id="d345-f2"/> closed its doors to the
                    public on December 31. Its Argentine employees, numbering some 20, are being
                    given employment by the post office, the state merchant fleet,
                        “Transradio”,<anchor n="52" xml:id="d345-f3"/> and Unión Telefónica; this is
                    being done at suggestion of Department of Labor.</p>
                <p>“All America”<anchor n="53" xml:id="d345-f4"/> and “Western Cables”<anchor n="54"
                        xml:id="d345-f5"/> were also requested to employ some of these persons, but
                    as yet have not complied.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Montevideo.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Armour</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d345-f1" n="49" xml:id="d345-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d345-f2" n="51" xml:id="d345-f2-content">
                <p>Italian-controlled communications system.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d345-f3" n="52" xml:id="d345-f3-content">
                <p>Transradio Internacional Argentina.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d345-f4" n="53" xml:id="d345-f4-content">
                <p>All America Cables and Radio Inc.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d345-f5" n="54" xml:id="d345-f5-content">
                <p>Presumably Western Telegraph Company, which handled cable traffic from Buenos
                    Aires to Europe.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
